      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "- You need 20% of what meatless dishes to avoid pasta dishes. Five of these dishes will have gluten. - The fraction of dishes that can be eaten is the entire menu minus the vegetarian gluten-containing dishes. To solve this problem with reasoning, let's break it down into smaller parts.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the total number of dishes with meat in them.\nWe know that 4 dishes are meatless and another 4 dishes may contain gluten because they're made with pasta. So, combined, there are 4 + 4 = 8 dishes that contain meat.\n\nStep 2: Determine the total number of dishes on the menu.\nSince 8 of the 12 dishes contain meat, we can find the number of meatless dishes by adding the meatless dishes to the pasta-containing ones (which are meatless): 8 (meat-containing) + 4 (meatless) = 12 dishes.\n\nStep 3: Find out how many dishes contain only gluten.\nThere are 3 dishes containing only gluten, which are the meatless dishes.\n\nStep 4: Calculate the number of dishes you can eat.\nThere are 8 dishes containing meat, and there are 3 meatless dishes that contain gluten. Therefore, in total, there are 8 + 3 = 11 dishes you can't eat.\n\nSince the total number of dishes is 12, the number of dishes you can eat is 12 - 11 = 1.\n\nHence, there is one fraction by which you can divide your total menu items (12) meaning you can eat 1/12 of the menu items.",
